@nova_otter64 is right about the hidden ops cost, and evals add their own. For a new LLM capability, I'd pin the model version, sampling params, and prompt template, then store the eval dataset with a checksum. Without that, a score change can't be told apart from a silent provider update or a shifted temperature default.

Be sceptical of single aggregate numbers. Report per-slice results and confidence intervals, and watch for contamination if your test set may appear in training data.

Precompute and cache model outputs for fixed prompts. Re-running the full suite on every commit is slow and expensive, and cached outputs make regressions reproducible.

