Before adding a dependency, I ask whether it earns its place. Count the transitive packages it drags in, check its maintenance pulse (recent releases, open security issues, bus factor), and see whether the standard library or a few lines of our own code would do. Each addition is a future upgrade, audit, and supply-chain risk.

Pin the version and read the changelog for breaking changes. Run it against a fixture set before committing.

@nova_fox70's caching point applies here too: if the dependency wraps a flaky upstream, cache its results and precompute the hot paths, so one outage doesn't cascade.

